17] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428147 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428148 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Separation of Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1033 1179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1034 1.517971234312146 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050905 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "701ed6eb-4bd0-5344-bf15-d9a52b59f3af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075118 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075118 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075118 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075118 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "385b574a-86cb-5cbc-a02b-25339e6092c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075119 #edb/ref 1080 "T^{′}(t) =-kT(T - 25)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075119 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075119 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8a1637b2-831b-5b2c-8230-97f9e2c57121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075120 #edb/ref 1080 "T^{′}(t) = k(T - 25)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075120 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075120 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c48530ee-0dd0-5b69-989e-1519b77c44cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075121 #edb/ref 1080 "T^{′}(t) = kT(T - 25)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075121 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075121 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07c2f09c-1d36-5b65-ab08-7c1af98a0a13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075122 #edb/ref 1080 "T^{′}(t) =-k(T - 25)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075122 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075122 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e0209ec-2c1f-5140-b7f5-c80513586152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075123 #edb/ref 1080 "T^{′}(t) = kT(T + 25)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075123 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075123 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "65ae7d88-d4f4-5879-9ad4-a09c82443506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078340 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078340 #edb/ref 1071 "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078340 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078340 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078341 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078341 #edb/ref 1071 "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078341 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078341 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078374 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078374 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078374 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078582 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1039 "Modeling Inhibited Decay" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078583 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078584 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1045 "Inhibited Decay Models With First-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "99731adb-b075-5d4b-865c-6112280a68cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078704 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078704 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078704 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078704 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078704 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fb765b0-7266-558a-8445-d5e7e8dcfb07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078704 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=2,a=500,$ and ${y}_{0}=600.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=400<a=500.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078706 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078706 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078706 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078706 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y>20$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078706 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0fe545c-1008-5ec6-a573-a706bc6b026b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078706 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=80,$ and $a=20.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>20$ for all $t>0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078707 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186075118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078707 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078707 #edb/ref 1071 "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t > 0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is $25^{∘}C$, which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078707 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "246dbe47-9137-5b96-af87-04ddded3bed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078743 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186275003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078743 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078743 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078743 #edb/ref 1071 "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078743 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b0ed3c5-b3aa-5668-ae2c-48b159839332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078743 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078744 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078744 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078744 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078744 #edb/ref 1071 "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is ${100}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078744 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "daa15db5-c1a1-535c-9031-79b2bb1e03e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078744 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that the temperature of the cup of coffee is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-0.05\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-\\int 0.05\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.05t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-0.05t+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-0.05t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the coffee was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the coffee after $t=6$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(6)=25+75{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 6}≈{81}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079538 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079538 #edb/ref 1061 584874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079538 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b8000c0-e0de-53fb-a0bd-c942ac0f5e81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079538 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079539 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079539 #edb/ref 1061 584875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079539 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21c46d35-42c5-51bc-8679-d81c11066483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079539 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079540 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079540 #edb/ref 1061 584880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079540 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2253199d-d2ca-53d8-a2f6-aa2bd0cd82fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079540 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079541 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079541 #edb/ref 1061 584881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079541 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f3084d6-abb3-5bf6-aedb-e65df0e1523b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079541 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079542 #edb/ref 1061 584884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079542 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ae0595c0-ae02-56d3-a449-bb3345ccbe25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079542 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079555 #edb/ref 1061 608082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7feac704-66a6-58a5-be02-6d1d595f029b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079619 #edb/ref 1064 "From the previous slide, the temperature of the thermometer is given by\n\n$$\nT = 20 + Ke^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ and $k$ are constants to be determined.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the water was $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0) = 100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =20+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =20+K \\\\ K & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following expression for $T(t){:}$\n\n$$\nT(t) = 20 + 80e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $k$ using the fact that $T(7)=60{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =20+80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ 40 & =80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-7k \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}(2) & =-7k \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature, in $^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ of the thermometer after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT = 20 + 80e^{-t\\ln(2)/7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, letting [math]t_1[/math] be the time when the thermometer reads [math]40^\\circ\\textrm{C},[/math] we have the following:\n\\[\n\\begin{align}\n40 &= 20 + 80e^{-kt_1} \\\\[5pt]\n20 &= 80e^{-kt_1} \\\\[5pt]\ne^{-kt_1} &= \\dfrac14 \\\\[5pt]\n-kt_1 &= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac14\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n-kt_1 &= -2\\ln\\left(2\\right) \\\\[5pt]\nt_1 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln\\left(2\\right)}{k} \\\\[5pt]\nt_1 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln\\left(2\\right)}{\\dfrac17\\ln\\left(2\\right)} \\\\[5pt]\nt_1 &= 14\n\\end{align}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the thermometer will read [math]40^\\circ\\textrm{C}[/math] after [math]14[/math] minutes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079619 #edb/ref 1065 14906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079619 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7811364e-9b00-5478-8f01-6f3ddfee6f58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079619 #edb/ref 1067 "Newton's Law of Cooling (Continued)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079671 #edb/ref 1064 "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited decay models, let’s look at a classic example: **Newton’s law of cooling**.\n\nThis law describes how the temperature of an object changes when it is placed in an environment with a different temperature. Formally, it states that\n\n> *The rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings.*\n\nThis leads to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nLet’s put this into action. Suppose a thermometer is placed in a pot of boiling water and reads $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ The pot is then placed in a $20^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ room, and the thermometer's reading begins to decrease.\n\nGiven that after $7$ minutes its reading is $60^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ let's model the thermometer's temperature after $t$ minutes.\n\nIn this case, the temperature of the surroundings is $T_S = 20^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, by Newton's law of cooling, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-20),\n$$\n\nwhere $T(t)$ is the temperature reading of the thermometer and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|T-20| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-20 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-20 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-20 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe'll determine the value of $K$ and $k$ in the next slide." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079671 #edb/ref 1065 4075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079671 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f58653bc-cafb-532a-b92d-8183054c181b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079671 #edb/ref 1067 "Newton's Law of Cooling" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079684 #edb/ref 1064 "In many real situations, a quantity decreases quickly at first, but then the decrease slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, consider a cup of hot coffee placed in a room. At first, the coffee cools rapidly because it is much hotter than the surrounding air. As time passes and the coffee temperature gets closer to room temperature, the cooling slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature rather than continuing to decrease.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited decay model**.\n\nAn *inhibited decay equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ decreases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as a lower bound. The solution decreases over time but never drops below $a,$ instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/1b/1b68f547db4090382b3fee626d029c619448b1c9adafe8b50312d162ff85e623.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited decay models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited decay, we factorise out the *negative* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 20-5P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited decay equation for $P > 4$ and $t > 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=5$ and $a=4$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = -5(P-4).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079684 #edb/ref 1065 5513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079684 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "da7cd802-d46b-546f-bdc3-8a783a8bb999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079684 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238171 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238171 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238171 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d0731f6-6530-57d1-8101-d583dce27200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238479 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238479 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238479 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30cfb7a5-89a3-5976-92cb-41428186daaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238574 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80(y+20)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238574 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860