db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243715 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243715 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186242933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243715 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "93430b60-75e1-51bd-a136-498bba75fcbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243745 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243745 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243745 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243745 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "aa52215b-9a2a-5e79-b263-c1fe67ce6a74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243845 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243845 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243845 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243845 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ec7d5808-b789-5047-9d21-ccfd0fbce57b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243972 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243972 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243972 #edb/ref 1071 "A turkey reaches ${145}^{∘}\\text{F}$ in the oven. After removing the turkey from the oven, the turkey's temperature $T(t)$ decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the turkey? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ba4e3cf-6728-56eb-8731-1748c4ffae5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243972 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243981 #edb/ref 1071 "A container of water at a temperature of ${90}^{∘}\\text{C}$ is placed in a room. The temperature $T(t)$ of the water decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the water temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f165134-d2dc-524e-9e90-e4a6ac3b7c02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243981 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243982 #edb/ref 1071 "The rate at which a pot of hot oil cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the oil? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "431988a0-8981-5d4d-9c5f-dd7ae32d4277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243982 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244024 #edb/ref 1071 "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${140}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $5$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b60de53-dea5-5dd2-908e-421b529445e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244024 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-0.1\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-\\int 0.1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-0.1t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-0.1t+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-0.1t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${140}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =28+K \\\\ K & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+112{e}^{-0.1t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=5$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(5)=28+112{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 5}≈{96}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244027 #edb/ref 1071 "A thermometer is taken out of a room where the air temperature is ${72}^{∘}F$  and brought outside where the temperature is ${15}^{∘}F,$ and its reading begins to decrease. After $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}F.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${25}^{∘}F?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred outside to ${15}^{∘}F.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39da46ca-e83e-580d-8039-34194e24ef5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244027 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={15}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-15| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-15>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-15 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-15 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-15 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =15+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${72}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=72,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =15+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 72 & =15+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=15+57{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=54,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54 & =15+57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{57} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57}) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${25}^{∘}F.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 15+57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ 57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =10 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{57} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${25}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57})}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})}≈9\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244028 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244028 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244028 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244028 #edb/ref 1071 "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a compressor of a refrigerator can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the compressor is ${65}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the compressor after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ff863d3-8088-512b-858b-b0b33302607f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244028 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that the temperature of the compressor of a refrigerator is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.12\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.12t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.12t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the compressor was ${65}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=65,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 65 & =21+K \\\\ K & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+44{e}^{-0.12t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the compressor after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=21+44{e}^{-0.12\\cdot 8}≈{38}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272570 #edb/ref 1080 "{83}^{∘}\\text{C}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272570 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272570 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1495237b-afbf-5205-a022-9f6acc5cbda9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272654 #edb/ref 1080 "12\\,\\text{min}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272654 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272654 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273200 #edb/ref 1080 "15\\,\\text{min}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273200 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273200 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5e7840ac-4dc2-5743-a317-909d0854aa2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273439 #edb